= The Secret of Willow Pond

Every morning before school, Nora walked to Willow Pond to feed the ducks. She carried a small paper bag of oats and a notebook, because Nora loved to draw. One cold Tuesday in October, she noticed something strange. The ducks were gathered at the far edge of the pond, and none of them came to eat.

"What's wrong with you today?" Nora asked. The ducks only quacked and stared at the reeds.

Nora put down her bag and crept closer. Between the tall, brown reeds she heard a thin, shaky sound: _peep, peep, peep._ A tiny duckling was tangled in a piece of old fishing line. It was shivering, and each time it tried to swim, the line pulled tighter.

Nora's heart raced. She wanted to rush in and grab it, but she remembered what her grandfather always said: "Slow hands help best." She took off her shoes, rolled up her jeans, and stepped into the icy water. Gently, she lifted the duckling and held it against her coat. With the tiny blade of her pencil sharpener, she carefully cut the line, one thread at a time.

When the duckling was free, it gave a happy peep and paddled back to its mother. The other ducks swarmed around it, quacking loudly, as if they were cheering.

Nora was late for school, and her socks were soaked. But when her teacher asked why, Nora only smiled and opened her notebook. On the page was a drawing of a small duckling, and beneath it she had written: "Today, patience was braver than hurry."

